URL Shortener
=============

A production-quality URL shortener built with .NET 10 / ASP.NET Core, following a clean,
layered architecture (API -> Application -> Domain, with Infrastructure implementing
Application/Domain abstractions).

Projects
--------
- src/UrlShortener.Domain          Core entity (ShortenedUrl) and business invariants. No
                                    framework dependencies.
- src/UrlShortener.Application     Use cases, DTOs, validation, and abstractions
                                    (IShortenedUrlRepository, IShortUrlCache, IShortCodeGenerator).
- src/UrlShortener.Infrastructure  EF Core + PostgreSQL persistence, Redis cache-aside,
                                    Base62 short-code generation, health checks, migrations.
- src/UrlShortener.Api             ASP.NET Core Web API: controllers, DI composition root,
                                    global exception handling, Swagger, health check endpoints.
- tests/UrlShortener.UnitTests     xUnit unit tests (validation, short-code generation,
                                    service behavior with mocks, architecture/dependency checks).
- tests/UrlShortener.IntegrationTests
                                    xUnit integration/E2E tests against real PostgreSQL and
                                    Redis containers via Testcontainers.

API
---
- POST /api/urls              Create a shortened URL. Body: { "url": "https://..." }.
                               Returns 201 with the short code / short URL. Not idempotent -
                               repeated calls for the same URL produce different short codes.
- GET  /{shortCode}            Redirect to the original URL (default: 302 Found, configurable).
                               Returns 404 if the short code does not exist.
- GET  /health/live             Liveness probe (process is up).
- GET  /health/ready             Readiness probe. PostgreSQL failures return 503; Redis
                                 failures are reported as Degraded and still return 200,
                                 since Redis is a soft dependency.
- /swagger                     Interactive API docs (Development environment only).

Running locally with Docker Compose
------------------------------------
    docker compose up --build

This starts PostgreSQL, Redis, and the API (http://localhost:8080). Database migrations are
applied automatically on startup.

Running locally without Docker
-------------------------------
1. Start PostgreSQL and Redis (e.g. via `docker compose up postgres redis`).
2. Update src/UrlShortener.Api/appsettings.Development.json connection strings if needed.
3. dotnet run --project src/UrlShortener.Api

Configuration
--------------
See appsettings.json. Key settings:
- ConnectionStrings:Postgres / ConnectionStrings:Redis
- UrlShortener:ShortCodeLength, MaxCollisionRetries, MaxUrlLength, RedirectStatusCode
- Cache:TtlHours, NegativeTtlSeconds, ConnectTimeoutMs, OperationTimeoutMs
- Database:ApplyMigrationsOnStartup (default true)

Tests
-----
    dotnet test tests/UrlShortener.UnitTests
    dotnet test tests/UrlShortener.IntegrationTests   # requires Docker (Testcontainers)

Database migrations
--------------------
    dotnet ef migrations add <Name> \
      --project src/UrlShortener.Infrastructure/UrlShortener.Infrastructure.csproj \
      --startup-project src/UrlShortener.Api/UrlShortener.Api.csproj \
      --output-dir Persistence/Migrations
